   167	## 7. Cost and operations
   168	
   169	**Work** (`experiments/v2/benchmark/timings.json`, 0.108 USD per core-hour): per cell a D2+H3 search (2,084 s), the
   170	evaluation on 200 seeds (96.0 s per 160 seeds, scaled: 120 s), the doubling check with its held-out evaluation (998 s),
   171	and two diagnostic profiles (60 s); churn slows an episode by 1.133 (p = 0.01) and 1.330 (p = 0.05) (section 1). 36
   172	cells plus the gate cell: **41.1 core-hours, 4.43 USD** at the benchmark price.
   173	
   174	**Memory** (`docs/revision/churn/churn_memory.py`): one 200-seed profile peaks at 981 MB without churn and 1,084 MB at
   175	p = 0.05; an evaluate or doubling task keeps four profiles (2.5 GB without churn, operations ledger), so about 2.8 GB
   176	under churn. Workers: search 16; evaluate, doubling and diagnostics 9 (about 25 GB, 23.3 GiB, below the pod's 28 GiB floor; an
   177	independent four-profile check at p = 0.05 in review peaked at 2,532 MiB). **Pod-side preflight** before the nine-worker
   178	stages (`memory_preflight`): the cgroup limit, the peak of one full evaluate task (four 200-seed profiles) in a child
   179	process, and the parent's own peak; workers = min(9, floor((0.9 x limit - parent) / task peak)), saved in
   180	`memory_preflight.json`; the driver refuses if no task fits.
   181	
   182	**Billed pod time** (one 16-vCPU pod, 0.56 USD per hour): the gate cell serially, then per rate the 18 searches in two
   183	waves of 16 and the evaluation, doubling and diagnostics in two waves of 9, plus 0.6 hours of preparation and idle:
   184	**5.95 hours, 3.33 USD**.
   185	
   186	**Budget** (author, 2026-11-10). The arm uses the shared ledger category `followup` (cap 22 USD for all follow-up runs).
